WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:09.332
Hello, my name is David Haas.

00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.966
And as you can see from the title,

00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:15.332
the subject is going to be cryo-cooling protein crystals

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:18.199
and other macromolecular crystals.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.232
The reason that crystals are cryo-cooled

00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:23.566
is so that they reduce radiation damage

00:00:23.567 --> 00:00:28.099
during synchrotron X-ray detection.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.466
I did this work on cryo-cooling

00:00:30.467 --> 00:00:33.166
the very first protein crystal back in 1967.

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:36.399
And then I left science in 1970

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.566
and didn't discover that, in fact,

00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:41.166
it was being used until just a few years ago.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:44.099
It turns out that the message

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.332
that I'm going to deliver today

00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:49.099
is in fact that research that you publish

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:50.899
-- your scientific research --

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.532
in peer-reviewed journals will in fact...

00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:56.466
could possibly be used years and years ahead,

00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:58.932
when you can't even foresee what will happen,

00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:01.166
and that's exactly what happened to me.

00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:04.632
The article I published in 1970

00:01:04.633 --> 00:01:06.666
was read

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.632
and has been used very successfully today

00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:13.999
by thousands of scientists around the world,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.699
with all types of synchrotron radiation.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.132
So, I'm going to tell you the story.

00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:20.932
And in fact, this is a picture taken of me

00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:22.999
when I worked in crystallography for five years,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.066
between 1965 and 1970.

00:01:25.067 --> 00:01:27.166
And then I joined industry.

00:01:27.167 --> 00:01:29.266
So, this is when I was a graduate student

00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:32.999
collecting data at the University of Buffalo.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.666
In 1965 I went on my first postoc,

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:40.166
and it was with David Phillips at the Royal Institution in London

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:43.499
And in fact you can see here the Lysozyme molecule.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.499
He had just solved the structure in 1965.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.432
And when I arrived he had suggested a project for me, and that

00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:55.499
I look at trying to reduce radiation damage in protein crystals.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.666
So I worked on that for a year and a half

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:01.332
at the Royal Institution with David Phillips, and of course,

00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:03.832
at that time, Sir Lawrence Bragg was actually the director,

00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:07.166
so he was very interested in the subject also.

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:10.332
A year and a half, I had no results whatsoever.

00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:12.666
And then I left the Royal Institution

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:15.166
and went to the Weizmann Institute soon after that,

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:17.499
and I'll tell you that story later on.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:21.332
But the important fact is that three dimensional structures

00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.666
of protein crystals had become very important, and its actually

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:28.999
created any entire new field called structural biology.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.866
In 1971, the Protein Data Bank was begun.

00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:36.666
And this is a picture of the various proteins.

00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:39.466
There were 9 protein structures solved in 1973.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:43.366
The Protein Data Bank has basically

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.366
become the heart of molecular biology.

00:02:45.367 --> 00:02:47.966
It is the resource where all the structures

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.466
of macromolecules are saved.

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:53.999
And today, there are more than 150,000 structures

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.966
that have been deposited,

00:02:57.967 --> 00:03:00.999
both of proteins, nucleic acids, DNA,

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.832
and a number of other macromolecules.

00:03:02.833 --> 00:03:06.232
One of the most remarkable things is that

00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:08.432
each year, now,

00:03:08.433 --> 00:03:10.299
about 10,000 new structures are being deposited.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:12.032
That's about 50 a day.

00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:14.666
And the data is being collected...

00:03:14.667 --> 00:03:17.166
the crystal data for X-rays

00:03:17.167 --> 00:03:20.199
is being collected on synchrotrons.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.732
So, I only discovered this just a few years ago.

00:03:23.733 --> 00:03:26.766
And when I went to my scientific meeting in 19...

00:03:26.767 --> 00:03:30.566
in 2015, I was given a copy of this magazine,

00:03:30.567 --> 00:03:34.932
which is a special issue of Journal of Molecular Biology --

00:03:34.933 --> 00:03:37.132
50 years of Protein Structure.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:40.332
And Max Perutz and, of course, John Kendrew

00:03:40.333 --> 00:03:42.266
were the two scientists who determined

00:03:42.267 --> 00:03:45.799
the very first protein structures.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.032
But today, it is 60 years -- 2019 --

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:52.666
since the first protein structures were solved,

00:03:52.667 --> 00:03:55.899
and it's been 52 years

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:58.899
since the first cryo-cooling of protein crystals.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.566
The Protein Data Bank has increased

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:07.699
in the number of structures dramatically.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.299
And what is surprising is that

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.766
about 90% of all these structures

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:14.632
in the Protein Data Bank

00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:16.299
have been cryo-cooled,

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.232
meaning that the data was collected

00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:21.166
at about 100 Kelvin.

00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:24.366
From the Journal of Molecular Biology issue,

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:29.766
there was a paragraph that said the three most significant things

00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:32.732
for the creation of the structural biology field

00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:35.499
are in fact the invention of the synchrotron;

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:37.599
the cryo-cooling of crystals,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.599
which permits them to be used;

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.832
and of course the high-speed computers

00:04:42.833 --> 00:04:45.266
and the remarkable computer programs and software

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:48.432
that have been developed to solve the structures.

00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:51.432
And just to amplify on that a little bit,

00:04:51.433 --> 00:04:55.032
this is the synchrotron at Brookhaven National Laboratory.

00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:58.199
There are 50 synchrotrons similar to this

00:04:58.200 --> 00:04:59.632
around the world,

00:04:59.633 --> 00:05:01.699
and they are available to all scientists.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.299
A synchrotron produces X-rays

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.166
about 10 million times more intense

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.366
than the vacuum tubes that I used.

00:05:08.367 --> 00:05:11.466
I mean... 10 million times... just remarkable.

00:05:11.467 --> 00:05:13.699
You can collect the data on a single crystal

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.399
in a matter of minutes,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.032
whereas in the 1960s,

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:21.499
it would take months or years to collect the data.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:23.366
And of course, the synchrotrons...

00:05:23.367 --> 00:05:26.532
now that they provide such an intense X-ray source,

00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:29.399
the crystals are protected by cryo-cooling,

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.799
and simply...

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.966
putting the crystals into liquid nitrogen.

00:05:33.967 --> 00:05:36.832
And that's exactly the way it was done in 1967.

00:05:36.833 --> 00:05:39.066
That I'm going to tell you about.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:41.466
And the third thing, of course,

00:05:41.467 --> 00:05:44.066
is the magnificent computer software and hardware

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.966
that is available today

00:05:45.967 --> 00:05:48.066
that permits three dimensional structures

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:49.932
not only to be solved

00:05:49.933 --> 00:05:51.666
but also the graphic interfaces

00:05:51.667 --> 00:05:53.566
that permit you to visualize them.

00:05:53.567 --> 00:05:56.132
But there is also a fourth area.

00:05:56.133 --> 00:06:01.199
And that is in fact the cloning to be able to produce proteins,

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.432
the purification of proteins,

00:06:03.433 --> 00:06:05.899
and a third area, which is the crystallization of proteins,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:08.966
which has become an entire field in itself,

00:06:08.967 --> 00:06:11.899
just the ability to be able to crystallize

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.366
these enormous macromolecules.

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:18.932
So, this really is a Back-to-the-Future story.

00:06:18.933 --> 00:06:22.299
This is the story that happened to me,

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.232
and that's what I want to relate to you.

00:06:24.233 --> 00:06:27.499
As young scientists or graduate students,

00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:29.499
basically,

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.499
you should realize that once you publish your scientific results

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.366
in a peer-reviewed journal,

00:06:33.367 --> 00:06:36.199
it could be used anytime in the future.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:39.132
And this Back-to-the-Future story

00:06:39.133 --> 00:06:40.932
that I'm telling you

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.432
is exactly what happened to me.

00:06:42.433 --> 00:06:44.499
50 years later,

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.866
I discovered that the work that I did...

00:06:46.867 --> 00:06:49.399
which I had totally forgotten about...

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.432
I discovered it was very valuable.

00:06:52.433 --> 00:06:56.466
So, in 1970,

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:58.599
Michael Rossman and I published the paper,

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:01.399
the second paper on cryo-cooling.

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:04.132
And the purpose in it

00:07:04.133 --> 00:07:06.166
was taking on lactate dehydrogenase crystal,

00:07:06.167 --> 00:07:07.699
cryo-cooled,

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.899
and we showed that, in fact,

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:12.099
the radiation damage at room temperature

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.899
is much more severe than it is at cryo-cooled.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.832
In fact, we showed that there's

00:07:18.833 --> 00:07:20.932
an improvement of ten times the life of the crystal

00:07:20.933 --> 00:07:23.066
at low temperatures.

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:24.966
Today, in synchrotrons,

00:07:24.967 --> 00:07:27.999
typically they consider the crystals

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.432
to have 70 times or more the lifetime

00:07:30.433 --> 00:07:32.632
in the X-ray beam.

00:07:32.633 --> 00:07:34.766
So, this is the first quantitative proof,

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:37.899
and it came out of this 1970 paper.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.032
So, let me just review with you,

00:07:40.033 --> 00:07:42.732
basically, my five years in crystallography

00:07:42.733 --> 00:07:44.766
and basic science.

00:07:44.767 --> 00:07:48.566
After graduating from University of Buffalo with David Harker,

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:52.266
we arranged for a postdoc

00:07:52.267 --> 00:07:54.199
at the Royal Institution of Great Britain.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.866
David Phillips had just finished working

00:07:56.867 --> 00:07:58.866
on the lysozyme structure.

00:07:58.867 --> 00:08:00.866
And in fact, Sir Lawrence Bragg

00:08:00.867 --> 00:08:03.366
was the director.

00:08:03.367 --> 00:08:05.099
After working a year and a half

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.599
on not having any results whatsoever

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.432
on reducing the radiation damage,

00:08:10.433 --> 00:08:12.332
I went to the Weizmann Institute

00:08:12.333 --> 00:08:14.832
because he (David Phillips)

00:08:14.833 --> 00:08:16.432
had taken a position at Oxford.

00:08:16.433 --> 00:08:18.732
And upon arriving at the Weizmann,

00:08:18.733 --> 00:08:20.799
I decided that I'd already cont...

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:22.632
I brought all my supplies with me,

00:08:22.633 --> 00:08:26.399
and I continued to work on the radiation damage,

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:27.832
probably...

00:08:27.833 --> 00:08:30.332
on the project for about three more months.

00:08:30.333 --> 00:08:32.732
In March of 1967,

00:08:32.733 --> 00:08:34.332
I decided that it was absolutely hopeless.

00:08:34.333 --> 00:08:36.432
I needed to find some other alternative.

00:08:36.433 --> 00:08:39.799
And what I did, in fact,

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:41.866
was made a list of all the alternatives

00:08:41.867 --> 00:08:43.599
that I could think of,

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.399
and I chatted with Wolfie Traub,

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:47.932
who was the director of the crystallography lab.

00:08:47.933 --> 00:08:49.932
And I concluded that cryo-cooling

00:08:49.933 --> 00:08:51.566
looked like the most promising.

00:08:51.567 --> 00:08:53.266
And as fate would have it,

00:08:53.267 --> 00:08:55.932
Wolfie Traub had just built

00:08:55.933 --> 00:08:58.466
a cryo-cooling apparatus about five years before,

00:08:58.467 --> 00:09:01.066
and it was sitting in the laboratory unused.

00:09:01.067 --> 00:09:03.599
So... and not only that,

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.732
he said I could use it,

00:09:05.733 --> 00:09:08.066
as well as the technician who was currently in the lab,

00:09:08.067 --> 00:09:09.966
who was the one who built it,

00:09:09.967 --> 00:09:11.799
and he would teach me how to use it.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.799
So, I was able to cryo-cool lysozome crystals

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:17.466
with cryoprotectants.

00:09:17.467 --> 00:09:21.499
They gave pristine diffraction patterns

00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:23.199
like this one.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.999
And then, in fact, after leaving it in the beam day after day after day,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.732
there was no change.

00:09:28.733 --> 00:09:31.366
And this was the first indication that I had

00:09:31.367 --> 00:09:34.032
that in fact there was some protection,

00:09:34.033 --> 00:09:36.299
or there was real radiation reduction,

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.899
in this lysozyme crystal.

00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:41.299
So, this paper was published

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:43.832
from the Weizmann.

00:09:43.833 --> 00:09:47.766
And then the Six-Day War occurred

00:09:47.767 --> 00:09:49.366
in June of 1967.

00:09:49.367 --> 00:09:51.466
I had already arranged with Michael Rossmann

00:09:51.467 --> 00:09:54.232
at Purdue University

00:09:54.233 --> 00:10:00.666
to go there, and I arrived in late 1967 at Purdue.

00:10:00.667 --> 00:10:04.932
My project that I wanted to work on with Michael

00:10:04.933 --> 00:10:07.432
was to show quantitatively

00:10:07.433 --> 00:10:12.466
that in fact radiation reduction is performed.

00:10:12.467 --> 00:10:14.666
And I was able to convince Michael,

00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:17.532
by showing him those wonderful precession pictures

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:19.199
from the Weizmann,

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.032
that in fact I could do the project.

00:10:23.033 --> 00:10:25.432
So, he loaned his diffractometer,

00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:29.866
and I built this equipment at Purdue in 1967,

00:10:29.867 --> 00:10:34.699
and we collected all the data during the summer of 1968.

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.832
After that, we wrote the paper.

00:10:37.833 --> 00:10:44.666
And this was published in April of 1969,

00:10:44.667 --> 00:10:46.299
as the Haas and Rossmann paper.

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:48.966
We submitted it in July of '68.

00:10:48.967 --> 00:10:51.732
And basically, the most important part of that paper

00:10:51.733 --> 00:10:54.966
was in fact this drawing

00:10:54.967 --> 00:10:56.632
of the radiation decay,

00:10:56.633 --> 00:10:58.966
which clearly shows

00:10:58.967 --> 00:11:01.832
a ten-fold improvement in radiation reduction damage

00:11:01.833 --> 00:11:06.266
in a cryo-cooled state.

00:11:06.267 --> 00:11:08.266
Soon after that,

00:11:08.267 --> 00:11:11.132
I had given a number of [talks] on this result

00:11:11.133 --> 00:11:16.099
and the cryo-cooling of protein crystals

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:17.799
at a number of scientific meetings,

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.066
and basically, at each of the meetings,

00:11:20.067 --> 00:11:22.266
there were no questions

00:11:22.267 --> 00:11:24.199
and a number of people said it's nice work

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:26.199
but it has absolutely no application.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:31.832
So, besides having a little depression from that,

00:11:31.833 --> 00:11:35.166
I also decided I really wasn't fit for academia,

00:11:35.167 --> 00:11:36.966
and I joined industry.

00:11:36.967 --> 00:11:40.032
So, in 1970 through basically 2015,

00:11:40.033 --> 00:11:44.166
I was out of the academic world.

00:11:44.167 --> 00:11:47.199
In 2015, as I said,

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:50.632
I went to the American Crystallographic Association meeting,

00:11:50.633 --> 00:11:57.232
and I started learning about structural biology,

00:11:57.233 --> 00:11:59.299
I was invited to attend

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:01.932
the X-ray methods course at Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory.

00:12:01.933 --> 00:12:05.799
And that was 2017.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.666
16 days of intense study.

00:12:09.667 --> 00:12:12.132
But the most remarkable part

00:12:12.133 --> 00:12:14.532
was that in the first week,

00:12:14.533 --> 00:12:16.299
we grew lysozyme crystals,

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:18.532
we cryo-cooled them,

00:12:18.533 --> 00:12:21.266
and then we sent him to the Brookhaven synchrotron

00:12:21.267 --> 00:12:23.332
and collected all the data

00:12:23.333 --> 00:12:25.932
in a matter of minutes.

00:12:25.933 --> 00:12:27.799
I can tell you,

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.666
one of my surprises was walking into the hutch...

00:12:29.667 --> 00:12:31.466
the X-ray hutch at the synchrotron,

00:12:31.467 --> 00:12:34.832
which is what this photograph is...

00:12:34.833 --> 00:12:37.499
basically, the instrumentation looked so fantastic,

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:39.866
and I didn't recognize any of it,

00:12:39.867 --> 00:12:42.066
except for the dewar,

00:12:42.067 --> 00:12:43.499
the liquid nitrogen dewar,

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:45.266
which basically was the same dewar

00:12:45.267 --> 00:12:49.966
that James Dewar had invented back in 1892.

00:12:49.967 --> 00:12:52.032
So, I didn't feel so bad about that.

00:12:52.033 --> 00:12:55.966
One of the remarkable coincidences

00:12:55.967 --> 00:12:59.932
is that in 1892,

00:12:59.933 --> 00:13:02.966
James Dewar did his work in exactly the same...

00:13:02.967 --> 00:13:04.699
at the Royal Institution,

00:13:04.700 --> 00:13:06.666
but it was also in exactly the same laboratory

00:13:06.667 --> 00:13:08.566
that I did my X-ray work

00:13:08.567 --> 00:13:11.832
when I was there in 1965.

00:13:11.833 --> 00:13:14.232
And I'm sure James Dewar

00:13:14.233 --> 00:13:16.832
would be very impressed about how his invention

00:13:16.833 --> 00:13:19.066
is being used today.

00:13:19.067 --> 00:13:21.966
So, the results of structural biology

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:24.532
and the Protein Data Bank

00:13:24.533 --> 00:13:26.899
basically produced an entire foundation

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:28.466
for molecular biology.

00:13:28.467 --> 00:13:31.366
And I'm sure many of you

00:13:31.367 --> 00:13:33.466
who are in the biological sciences

00:13:33.467 --> 00:13:35.432
have come to appreciate the value

00:13:35.433 --> 00:13:38.266
of having these remarkable three-dimensional structures

00:13:38.267 --> 00:13:40.499
and data available.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:45.032
So, in my second presentation,

00:13:45.033 --> 00:13:46.999
I'm gonna tell you about how my postdoctoral work,

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:48.966
50 years later,

00:13:48.967 --> 00:13:52.199
has contributed substantially to structure-based drug design,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.066
which has led to many of the drugs

00:13:56.067 --> 00:13:59.999
that have been used for the HIV epidemic,

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.466
and as I will specifically show you,

00:14:02.467 --> 00:14:04.099
the Lazarus effect,

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.799
which is one of...

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:09.099
one of the phenomena that happened in 1996

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:13.899
during the HIV epidemic.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:17.399
I'd also like to thank the people

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.866
who helped me over the years:

00:14:19.867 --> 00:14:22.799
as you might gather, David Phillips,

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.932
who suggested the original project

00:14:25.933 --> 00:14:28.732
of reducing radiation damage;

00:14:28.733 --> 00:14:32.666
Wolfie Traub... if he had not had, in his laboratory,

00:14:32.667 --> 00:14:34.366
the cryo-cooling equipment that I used,

00:14:34.367 --> 00:14:36.466
it probably never would have gotten done...

00:14:36.467 --> 00:14:39.799
probably 10, 20, 30 years later;

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:42.599
Michael Rossman, I think who recognized

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:45.066
the importance of having a single crystal

00:14:45.067 --> 00:14:47.666
that showed reduced radiation damage

00:14:47.667 --> 00:14:49.732
so you could collect all your data;

00:14:49.733 --> 00:14:52.299
and then I especially want to thank

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.332
Elspeth Garman,

00:14:54.333 --> 00:14:57.066
who was one of the experts in radiation damage

00:14:57.067 --> 00:15:00.166
and has taught me, in a matter of months,

00:15:00.167 --> 00:15:03.032
what I did not learn in 50 years.

00:15:03.033 --> 00:15:06.032
So, the final sentence should be

00:15:06.033 --> 00:15:08.032
"science for the benefit of humanity,"

00:15:08.033 --> 00:15:10.532
which is exactly what I see structural biology

00:15:10.533 --> 00:15:12.166
has contributed.

00:15:12.167 --> 00:15:30.767
Thank you.

